# **The gauntlet's work order.** One line per violation, as
# `room | rule | offender` — see `gauntlet.rs`, "The docket".
#
# This file is the deliverable of the harness pass and the input to the
# fixing pass. Strike a line, run `cargo test -p cabin`, and
# `the_gauntlet_finds_exactly_the_docket` will tell you whether the thing
# is actually gone: a line struck too early fails, and a defect fixed
# without striking its line fails too.
#
# Regenerate wholesale with:
#   ./target/debug/space-trucking --gauntlet-docket
# and read the numbers behind any line with:
#   ./target/debug/space-trucking --gauntlet
#
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.
#
# **`furniture-seated` is empty, and its ten came off in two pieces.**
#
# Five were the comet's quarried face: blocks of ice that said the
# starboard wall was what they were cut from and stopped between 22 and
# 110 mm in front of it. The one-line cure was wrong and the source said
# so — the frozen block already stood hard against that wall with one of
# these inside it, and five slabs run out to one plane share their back
# face wherever they cross. A quarried face is one body now: the back of
# the cut meets the wall, and the five blocks are what was left standing
# on it.
#
# The other five were one vocabulary defect wearing five station badges:
# a `Shape::Ring` laid flat "on the deck" — a moon pool's collar, a core
# cradle, three coiled hoses. A torus's drawn tube is 18% of the frame
# `Fitting::half` declares and the containment law was reading the frame,
# so a hoop whose tube met the deck had a frame through the floor.
# `poi::Shape::fill` says the fraction once now; `Fitting::span` reads
# the body through it, so a claim on space belongs to the thing that
# occupies it; and `Fitting::meeting` writes the joint instead of the
# decimal.
#
# **What is left is one re-authoring.** Twenty cargo kinds are drawn as
# bas-reliefs centred in their own cell face —
# the 2D console's glyphs, given depth and never given a floor — so each
# of them stands somewhere between one and fifteen centimetres above the
# deck it is berthed on, with nothing under it. `rig-seated` is the
# question that found them; docs/BAY.md has been carrying the answer as
# deferred work since the net landed ("every kind re-authors its extent
# for the plane it mounts"), and the couch and the cabinet are the two
# that have had it done. The lines come off one kind at a time, and a
# kind comes off by being drawn standing rather than by being shoved
# down: a chit translated onto the deck is a playing card balanced on
# its edge, which is a worse picture than the one it replaces.
#
# The rest of this note is the history of how the file got emptied, and
# it is worth keeping while there is anything on it at all. Three layers
# of the world have been described and swept, and each of them was
# invisible until somebody wrote the description. What that history says
# is that the next defect is in the layer nobody has thought to describe
# yet, not in the ones below — so the useful thing to do with a short
# file is to read docs/GAUNTLET.md's account of what the sweep can and
# cannot see, and go and look at something it cannot.
#
# The rooms were the first layer. Their lines were three families and one
# cause, and they came off when a calling room got a deck of its own to
# lend (`Tile::Staging`, docs/ROOMS.md).
#
# The cargo was the second. Thirty-two kinds had never been swept at all,
# because a rig was built straight into a live world and there was
# nothing to measure; there is a description now (`pieces::parts`), and
# the sweep found sixty-one things in it — fifty-nine cured on the spot
# and two that took a kind re-drawn. Those two were the floor lamp's base
# disc and the very mysterious crate's core, each hanging out of
# `pieces::RIG_NEAR..RIG_FAR`, and the parts moved rather than the band:
# the lamp's plate, pole, shade and bulb stand on one axle now instead of
# three, and the crate's core is sunk to its own radius so its crown is
# the band's own face.
#
# Widening the band was the other cure, and it prices worse than it looks:
# the two spill at OPPOSITE ends — the lamp 0.069 m behind the near face,
# the crate 0.073 m past the far one — so a band that cured either costs
# about 15% more air on every wall berth in the game, and a band that
# cured both costs 29%. That is a systemic price for a local defect. The
# measurement that settled it for good is that neither kind can be
# berthed on a wall at all — every kind's mount is Floor unless it is
# tagged otherwise, and a deck berth's air is its own cell's column — so
# neither was ever the berth-measured-too-shallow defect the line was
# written up as. Both were a body hanging out of the box the carry tell
# wraps a carried rig in, which is defect enough and is what the finding
# says now.
#
# The band did move later, and for a reason of its own rather than to
# swallow a part: the world is built of the cargo grid and the band was
# nine tenths of a cell. It is one cell now, wearing the same `BAY_FIT`
# the width and the height wear. It cost a wall berth 31 mm of room
# (0.466 m of reach becomes 0.497 m) and cost this file nothing, because
# the reading it feeds was measuring the band's thickness where the
# reach was what mattered.
#
# The doorways were the third. Every piece of hardware in a seam was
# built straight into the world, so the sweep walked through a dozen
# doorways a room without being able to name one thing in any of them —
# which is how a latch came to be drawn twice at one transform and to
# photograph two ways. A doorway describes itself now
# (`room::seam_parts`) and is swept from both of its sides, because a
# seam is drawn once by the room with the lower id and stands on the
# boundary both of them own. Fifty-three fights came out of that first
# pass and all fifty-three were cured, in the same three idioms the
# cargo's were: a body written down twice (the latch, the jamb lamp, and
# a corner cell's tread laid once per doorway instead of once per cell);
# two members of one fitting cut to one length so they cross (the lintel
# against both stiles, the hatch coaming against itself at four corners);
# and a part run out flush with the edge its parent was cut at (the frame
# and the coaming against the very plane the aperture was punched on,
# cured by straddling it — half a jamb over the line, which is what a
# lining is for).
#
# The sentences all three closed are kept said as tests in `gauntlet.rs`,
# because a docket shrinks and a law does not.
#
# The fourth layer was the LATTICE itself, and it is the first one that
# was not invisible for want of a description — every room's box, every
# wall and every doorway could always be enumerated. It was invisible for
# want of a QUESTION. Nothing had ever asked whether the fabric was
# actually on the grid it was supposed to be built out of, and it was
# not: a deckhead at 4.109 cells, a wall thickness and a chart trim
# chosen by eye, a hand-measured cabin hull two centimetres off its own
# box, and two rooms mating flush so that their hulls stood through each
# other by two wall thicknesses at every seam on every ship. That last
# one is the defect the owner reported three times as the incinerator
# clipping into the main cabin, and no pass that looked at placements
# could have found it, because no placement was wrong.
#
# `grid-fits` is the question. It found 126 things and all 126 were
# cured; docs/GAUNTLET.md carries what they were and what the two named
# exemptions are. The lesson from the three layers below still stands and
# now has a companion: the next defect is either in the layer nobody has
# described yet, or in the question nobody has thought to ask about a
# layer that is fully described.
#
# `part-seated` was the second of the second kind, and it needed no new
# description at all: a rig's parts have been enumerable since
# `pieces::parts` landed. What was missing was that every rule asked
# about a part and the WORLD — the band it is composed in, the plane it
# fights, the cells it draws inside, the direction its own name claims —
# and none asked whether a part meets the other part of the same rig its
# own name says holds it up. Three did not: the couch's four feet stopped
# 8.7 mm short of the seat they carry, the destination preview's glass
# floated 9.3 mm off its bezel (in the played build only — the headless
# fallback body overlapped the brass, so the one build people look at was
# the one that showed it), and the porthole's sky pane missed its own
# bolt ring by 9.3 mm. All three were cured; a joint is spent explicitly
# now, one ladder step in or one step proud (`pieces::GLAZE`).
#
# `rig-seated` is the third of that second kind, and the closest sibling
# `part-seated` has: the same joint, one plane down and outside the rig.
# Every family before it measured a part against the world's numbers —
# the band a rig is composed in, the plane it fights, the cells it draws
# inside — or against another part of the same rig. None measured the rig
# against the one thing its berth actually promises: the deck it stands
# on, the deckhead it hangs from, the wall it is screwed to. `face-fits`
# comes nearest and looks the other way by design, forgiving a centimetre
# of a sole BURIED in its own deck and asking nothing at all about a
# sole a hand's breadth above it.
#
# Two of the twenty-two it found were cured on the spot and both were the
# same shape as the wall lamp's pad, which is the defect this class keeps
# producing: a fitting composed at the middle of the band instead of at
# the plane it is bolted to. The ceiling lamp's canopy stopped 8.4 mm
# under the deckhead it is screwed to, and the porthole's whole
# assembly — glass, bolt ring and six studs — hung 32.6 mm out in the air
# in front of its wall, with the room's own paint visible under the brim.
# Both are drawn from their own plane now, buried one ladder step into it
# (`pieces::SOLE_BURY`), which is the same step a pane spends on a bezel.
#
# `furniture-seated` closed that, and it is the fourth of the second
# kind. What the family before it could not ask about was a station's own
# furniture and a doorway's own hardware: a rig declares the chart it is
# berthed on because the sim berths it, and a `Fitting` is a fraction of
# a room's box and a seam part is world units off a site, and neither of
# them declared anything at all. So a beacon bolted to thin air and a
# latch floating in front of a wall were invisible to every rule in the
# file, and both were found by a player looking at the screen.
#
# The description is a `Seat` on `poi::Fitting` and on `room::SeamPart` —
# a face of the room's own box, or another body by the name it declares —
# and the family reads the claim back exactly as `part-seated` does.
# Two hundred and sixty-four claims are declared across fifteen stations
# and every doorway in the game, and curing what they found moved 133 of
# the 292 fittings those rooms hang, every rivet on every shut door, and
# one bracket that was not there at all — the Hermitage's third ledge had
# no corbel under it. The shapes repeat, and they are worth naming: four
# rivets on each of three shut leaves, drawn a plate thickness out past
# the BACK of the plate they fasten and squarely behind it from every
# stance in the room; three seized pendants sharing one stem length at
# three heights, two of them hanging off nothing; a candle 60 mm over the
# sill it stands on; three cryo cores 55 mm under their rail and five
# blackout tins 56 mm under theirs; a stack of hull plate 105 mm off the
# deck; a main riser that ran "deck to cornice" from 176 mm above the
# deck; three pillars "floor to deckhead" that reached neither; and a
# dozen plates, patches, plaques and boards standing between 12 and 77 mm
# off the walls they are bolted to. The ten that are left are the two
# classes above.







